speechtext:
Hon. W. R. MOTHERWELL (Minister of Agriculture) moved that the House go into committee to consider the following proposed resolution:
Resolved, That it is expedient to amend The Dairy Industry Act, chapiter 7 of the statutes of 1914, for the following purposes:-
(a)	to make further provision for definitions of Dominion Analyst, illegal dairy product, oleomargarine, package and renovated butter;
(b)	to prevent the use of oil as an adulterant in butter, and to prohibit the keeping of foreign fats that might be used for adulterating purposes on the premises of any person engaged in the manufacture or manipulation of butter;
(c)	to avoid the necessity of proving that foreign fats have been added to skimmed milk in cases of
infraction;
(d)	to provide heavier penalties for violation of the act;
(e)	to authorize an inspector in addition to obtaining access to premises, to take samples, seize and remove illegal dairy products;
(f)	to empower the Governor in Council to make regulations respecting the seizure and confiscation of materials and packages used illegally, and to control the size and dimensions of cheese hoops and cheese and butter boxes; and
(g) to permit of the registered number of any package being accepted as prima facie evidence of the identity of the manufacturer, and that costs of analysis shall, upon conviction, be included in the costs, of prosecution.
Motion agreed to and the House went into committee, Mr. Gordon in the chair.

Just a formal extension
for two years. Since the act was passed the time for completion of the road and the earning of the subsidy has been extended^ I might explain here that a peculiarity, which only came to my notice a few days ago,

occurred two years since. At that time this House passed an extension of the act extending the time for the completion of the line, but it never got over to the Senate at all. We cannot blame the Senate for killing that bill; it never saw it. I only discovered the fact quite recently. The law officers of the crown say this bill will reinstate the line and give it two years extension; it is purely formal. There is no doubt in the world it was originally intended that this particular piece should be constructed; and it is a portion of the line included in the agreement made between the Dominion government and the government of the province of New Brunswick, I think along in 1916, by which the latter government undertook to construct a line and the Intercolonial, as its share, undertook to operate it. The St. John Valley railway is now operated by the Intercolonial, and while the province of New Brunswick has to pay a quarter of a million dollars interest on the cost of construction, it also costs the Dominion government a sum approximating a quarter of a million dollars every year for operation. It is a situation that is serious both from the railway standpoint and from the standpoint of the province of New Brunswick. As to the correspondence and the oral negotiations urging the taking over of the St. John Valley railway, I may say that the government of New Brunswick has urged very strongly that it is the duty of the Dominion government to take over this piece of line. Up to date, so far as I know and I think I would know, the negotiations have not come to a point where the federal government has committed itself to take over the line. It is thought that if the branch lines proposed by the Canadian National, and I believe to be constructed, will in a measure, quite a large measure, increase the traffic on the St. John Valley railway-
